{"items":[{"targetId":"n0000282","injections":[{"type":"citation_needed","text":"Figure 5 is a numbered visual result whose axes, conditions, and conclusion must be obtained from the referenced source figure.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"fills","reason":"The dependent claim needs the figure-specific experimental evidence rather than a generic definition."}]},{"targetId":"n0000283","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A square matrix X is skew-symmetric when Xᵀ = −X, so every diagonal entry is zero.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"fills","reason":"This defines the constrained matrix used before the Cayley transform."}]},{"targetId":"n0000284","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A computational problem is intractable when its resource cost grows too rapidly with input size for practical exact solution at the relevant scale.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"fills","reason":"This explains why factorial parameterizations become unusable as stream count grows."}]},{"targetId":"n0000285","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The logarithm log_b(x) is the exponent y such that bʸ = x.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"fills","reason":"This supplies the mathematical meaning needed for logarithmic complexity or transformed quantities."}]},{"targetId":"n0000286","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Factorization represents an object as a structured combination of smaller factors, often reducing storage or computation by exploiting that structure.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"fills","reason":"This explains the efficiency motivation of Kronecker-factorized methods."}]},{"targetId":"n0000287","injections":[{"type":"citation_needed","text":"Table 1 is a numbered tabular comparison whose rows, columns, experimental conditions, and reported values must be read from the source table.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"fills","reason":"The dependent evidence requires the table-specific comparison, not an inferred result."}]},{"targetId":"n0000288","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Big-O notation O(f(d)) denotes an asymptotic upper growth rate: beyond some size, the quantity is bounded by a constant multiple of f(d).","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"fills","reason":"This enables comparison of the methods' scaling laws."}]},{"targetId":"n0000289","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The tensor product combines two vector spaces or matrices into a larger structured object whose dimensions multiply; for matrices, it is commonly realized as the Kronecker product.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"fills","reason":"This supplies the operation underlying Kronecker-factorized residual mixing."}]},{"targetId":"n0000290","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"An orthostochastic matrix is a doubly stochastic matrix whose entries are the elementwise squares of an orthogonal matrix.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"fills","reason":"This defines the expressivity boundary reached at s = 1."}]},{"targetId":"n0000291","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Quadratic cost grows proportionally to the square of a size parameter, such as O(d²) for d streams.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"fills","reason":"This explains the stated computational scaling."}]},{"targetId":"n0000292","injections":[{"type":"citation_needed","text":"Section 5's convergence result must be read from the source to determine the metric, convergence criterion, compared methods, and measured outcome.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"fills","reason":"A section reference does not itself specify the empirical convergence evidence."}]},{"targetId":"n0000293","injections":[{"type":"citation_needed","text":"Section 5.1's synthetic stream-mixing result must be read from the source to determine the task construction, loss metric, and numerical performance comparison.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"fills","reason":"The named subsection is required to interpret its experiment-specific claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000294","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A synthetic toy-model experiment evaluates a controlled, simplified task designed to isolate a mechanism rather than reproduce full real-world data complexity.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"fills","reason":"This clarifies the evidential scope of the stream-mixing evaluation."}]},{"targetId":"n0000013","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A factorially sized parameterization must represent one component for every permutation of d streams, whereas a factorized parameterization reuses smaller components.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This connects the exactness–expressivity trade-off to the two representations' structure."}]},{"targetId":"n0000014","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Cubic scaling is tractable relative to factorial scaling because increasing d adds polynomially many parameters and operations instead of enumerating all d! permutations.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"supports","reason":"This explains the practical importance of the proposed scaling claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000015","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Composing a structured Kronecker mixer with an additional expressive doubly stochastic mixer can enlarge the reachable set without abandoning the structured computation of the Kronecker component.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains how similar FLOP cost can coexist with recovered expressivity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000017","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Reaching the theoretical minimum loss means the learned mixer attains the best loss allowed by the experiment's specified objective and target construction.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This clarifies what the reported synthetic result establishes."}]},{"targetId":"n0000019","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Treating stream count d as model capacity means increasing the number of parallel residual pathways available for learned mixing, analogous to increasing width or depth.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This connects the method's scaling claim to a familiar capacity dimension."}]},{"targetId":"n0000021","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff polytope is the set of square doubly stochastic matrices: nonnegative matrices whose rows and columns each sum to one.","densityScore":0.92,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This supplies the central constrained geometry named by the claim."},{"type":"mechanism","text":"A parameterization maps unconstrained optimization variables into a constrained matrix set so every produced matrix satisfies the required structure by construction or projection.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains why geometric parameterization affects model behavior."}]},{"targetId":"n0000022","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A doubly stochastic attention matrix preserves total probability mass across both source and destination positions because each row and column sums to one.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This connects the constraint to its probability-trajectory interpretation."}]},{"targetId":"n0000023","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Sinkhorn normalization alternates row and column rescaling to move a nonnegative matrix toward doubly stochastic form.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This identifies the mechanism behind Sinkhorn-style constrained attention."}]},{"targetId":"n0000025","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"With d residual streams, a mixing matrix determines how each output stream combines all current streams while an identity-compatible setting preserves an unchanged information path.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains how more streams increase connectivity without removing residual behavior."}]},{"targetId":"n0000027","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"For a skew-symmetric matrix X, the Cayley transform Q = (I − X)(I + X)⁻¹ produces an orthogonal matrix whenever I + X is invertible.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This isolates the first construction step in the dense figure description."},{"type":"mechanism","text":"Squaring the entries of an orthogonal matrix yields a nonnegative orthostochastic matrix whose rows and columns sum to one.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"supports","reason":"This explains how orthogonal structure induces double stochasticity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000028","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Restricting residual mixing to doubly stochastic matrices prevents arbitrary amplification or loss of aggregate stream mass, which can make repeated mixing more stable.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"supports","reason":"This explains the stated stability benefit of the constraint."}]},{"targetId":"n0000031","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"An approximate constraint can introduce a small per-layer violation that compounds through repeated layers, while an exact parameterization prevents that particular accumulation mechanism.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains why exactness matters in deep compositions."}]},{"targetId":"n0000032","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff–von Neumann theorem states that every doubly stochastic matrix can be expressed as a convex combination of permutation matrices.","densityScore":0.93,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This provides the theorem required to understand mHC-lite's representation."}]},{"targetId":"n0000033","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Factorial growth d! exceeds every fixed-base exponential cᵈ as d increases, so enumerating all permutation weights rapidly becomes impractical.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This precisely explains the scalability failure of the full permutation representation."}]},{"targetId":"n0000034","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A Kronecker product restricts a large mixing matrix to combinations determined by smaller factors, reducing cost but excluding most unconstrained doubly stochastic matrices.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This directly explains the efficiency–expressivity trade-off."}]},{"targetId":"n0000036","injections":[{"type":"transition","text":"The hyperparameter s controls a continuum of parameterization families, allowing a user to choose a cheaper restricted region or a more expressive region of the doubly stochastic set.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This turns the abstract efficiency–expressivity balance into a tunable design decision."}]},{"targetId":"n0000039","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"A controlled stream-mixing task isolates whether a parameterization can reach target mixing transformations, while a GPT-style model tests whether that behavior transfers to a realistic training workload.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This distinguishes the complementary purposes of the two evaluations."}]},{"targetId":"n0000041","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Reachability is the ability of a parameterization to represent or closely approach a desired matrix within its target constrained set.","densityScore":0.85,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This defines the experimental advantage claimed over competing parameterizations."}]},{"targetId":"n0000043","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"An O(d³) method remains polynomial in stream count, whereas the d! components required by a full permutation mixture become dominant even at moderate d.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"supports","reason":"This establishes why the reported asymptotic comparison matters."}]},{"targetId":"n0000045","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Batched linear solves and tensor contractions are standard high-level tensor operations commonly supported by general-purpose deep-learning runtimes.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains why avoiding custom CUDA kernels lowers implementation burden."}]},{"targetId":"n0000046","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A construction is exact when every valid parameter value produces a matrix satisfying the stated constraint, rather than only approaching it after iterative optimization.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This clarifies the claim of exact double stochasticity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000047","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"At s = 1 the construction is restricted to orthostochastic matrices, while increasing s enlarges the reachable portion toward the interior of the doubly stochastic set.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains the claimed interpolation behavior."}]},{"targetId":"n0000049","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Using go-mHC with KromHC can assign structured Kronecker mixing to large-scale composition while using go-mHC to recover mixing patterns excluded by the Kronecker structure.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains the proposed complementarity."}]},{"targetId":"n0000052","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"An identity skip path lets gradients bypass a nonlinear residual branch, reducing the need for gradients to survive only repeated transformed layers.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains the foundational role of skip connections."}]},{"targetId":"n0000056","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A multi-stream residual architecture maintains several parallel hidden-state streams and learns matrices that mix information among them between layers.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This defines the architecture generalized by Hyper-Connections."}]},{"targetId":"n0000059","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Repeated multiplication by unconstrained mixing matrices can magnify or attenuate hidden-state and gradient components, so identity preservation is no longer guaranteed.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains the source of the stated gradient-instability risk."}]},{"targetId":"n0000061","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The spectral norm of a matrix is its largest singular value and bounds the maximum factor by which the matrix can amplify a vector's Euclidean norm.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This defines the quantity used to motivate constrained gradient updates."}]},{"targetId":"n0000064","injections":[{"type":"transition","text":"Constraining ℋ_l^res to converge to B_d replaces arbitrary stream mixing with nonnegative, row- and column-normalized mixing.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This makes the architecture-level consequence of the mHC constraint explicit."}]},{"targetId":"n0000071","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Convex-combination weights are nonnegative and sum to one, so combining permutation matrices this way remains inside the doubly stochastic set.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This explains why the theorem-based mHC-lite construction preserves the constraint."}]},{"targetId":"n0000075","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The factor C in O(d²C) denotes a component-dependent cost multiplier, so quadratic dependence on d does not by itself determine the full runtime.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"This qualifies the reported KromHC complexity expression."}]}]}
